# KTP OCR — consent first (D7)

Sprint doc: `docs/sprints/phase-3-patient-ktp-roi-ocr-clinical-pilot-1.md`.
Protocol: `docs/runbooks/ktp-camera-ocr-supervised-pilot-protocol.md` (§0 D7, §3, §4, Appendix A).
Extends rules 177 (OCR is a suggestion), 178 (who may use it) and 179 (how it reads).

1. **The approved wording is canonical.** `config/patient_ktp_ocr_consent.php`
   holds the D7 text verbatim with a `version` (`D7-2026-10-10`). It is read only
   by `App\Modules\Patient\Support\KtpOcrConsent`. Never paraphrase, shorten or
   translate it in a view, a printout or a message.
2. **Changing the wording is a new owner approval AND a code change** with a new
   `version` — never an environment edit. The file reads no `env()` (tested).
3. **Consent before capture and before processing.** The camera does not open,
   and OCR does not read an image from ANY source (camera, scanner agent, manual
   file), until the KTP holder's explicit "yes" is recorded on the page. No answer
   is not a yes; a "no" is an answer — OCR does not ask again on its own; only the
   operator pressing the camera button again (the holder changed their mind)
   asks anew. A new photo cancels an unanswered question.
4. **The server boundary is the parse endpoint.** `ParseKtpOcrRequest` refuses OCR
   text without `consent` accepted and the CURRENT `consent_version` (422). The
   pilot gate still runs first, so an operator outside the pilot gets the same 404
   with or without a consent field — consent is never an oracle.
5. **Unusable wording fails closed.** A missing version/title or a broken
   paragraph makes the wording unusable: no partial text, no "agree" button, an
   empty accepted-version list (every parse refused), and the status command's
   `--strict` exits 2 while the flag is on.
6. **Declining keeps manual registration complete.** The scanner/manual document
   upload and typed registration need no consent field and are unchanged. A
   declined photo is stored as a plain document, with no OCR request sent.
7. **No consent record is invented.** The KTP holder's written consent stays on
   the clinic's paper form (protocol Appendix A). The application stores no consent
   row, writes no audit row for it, and must NOT borrow `trx_rme_visit_consents`
   (that is treatment consent with a different purpose and lifecycle). The page's
   answer lives in that page only; clearing the photo resets it ("Ulangi" keeps it).
8. **An operator statement is not the holder's signature.** The on-screen answer is
   the operator attesting that consent was given; it never replaces the written form.
9. **Measure without identifiers.** The screen's "Catatan pilot" line carries counts
   and field KEYS only (card found, corners adjusted, boxes moved, retries per field,
   first read time), is shown on screen only and never sent. The capture sheet and
   `tools/ktp-ocr-pilot/summarize.mjs` reject anything identifier-shaped (6+ digit
   runs, e-mail addresses, dates outside the date column) by row and column, never
   echoing the value. Keep the sheet off chat, e-mail and AI/MCP services.
10. **Privacy review is a precondition the owner owns.** D7 delegates retention,
    data-subject rights and the privacy contact to the clinic privacy policy
    (UU 27/2022 Pasal 21(1) d, f, g). Until that policy is available to the KTP
    holder, record `PRIVACY_REVIEW=PENDING`; never write a policy on the clinic's
    behalf and present it as approved.
11. **Never claim the device pilot passed from synthetic or browser-only runs.**
    `REAL_CAMERA_TEST` and `REAL_KTP_TEST` are MEASURED on site under consent, or
    `PENDING`. The summariser reports `PENDING` below 30 consented captures, `NO_GO`
    on any confident wrong NIK, and `METRICS_PASS` at most — clinical GO is the
    owner's decision on the full protocol §5 list.
12. **The pilot period is not extended by this rule or by any release.** The
    2026-10-16 end date stands; at period end disarm or record a fresh owner decision
    (rule 178 §16).
13. **The live field overlay is image processing too** (rule 181): it starts only
    once the camera is open — which already needs the "yes" — re-checks the answer
    on every analysis, and stops at once when the camera closes or the answer is
    withdrawn. No frame is analysed before the "yes", ever.
